title: Revert
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: overview
aliases: revert
summary: A revert undoes one past revision or operation in place — writing back the state it replaced as a new revision — while everything after it stays.

Rollback moves HEAD: it undoes everything back to a point in time, and what it passes
over is undone because it is ''in the way'', not because anyone objected to it. Right for
"take me back to lunchtime"; wrong for "that one rename was a mistake" — above all in a
watched repository, where dozens of unrelated watcher revisions may have landed since.

A ''revert'' answers the second question. It undoes one revision, or one operation,
''without moving HEAD'': the daemon computes the state that change replaced and writes it
back as an ordinary new revision. The log grows by one entry saying so, and nothing
leaves it — the mistake and its correction are both on the record, which is what an
audit log is for.

A revert is an ordinary write in every other respect: it can itself be reverted
(restoring the original change), rolled back across, pruned and trimmed. What marks it is
`reverts_op_id` on each of its operations, naming what it undid.

Not every change can be reverted on its own: a later one may have overwritten what it
wrote, leaving no meaningful "before" to restore ([[Operation dependencies]]). Undo reverts
for you when the watcher has written since your change ([[Undo and redo]]).

